  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1044 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nONGOING FLOODING WITH MORE RAINFALL THIS WEEKEND:\n\nThe low pressure system responsible for pulling in the tropical\nmoisture that resulted in the abundant rainfall the last couple\ndays is sitting over southern California and Nevada. This low \npressure system is expected to move into Arizona by Saturday \nafternoon and lift northeast across Utah and western Colorado \nduring the day on Sunday and exiting to the northeast by Monday \nmorning. As this happens, the low pressure system will pull \nanother round of moisture around the low from the southeast on \nSaturday. The latest hi-res CAMs indicate convection developing \nacross southeast Utah and southwest Colorado overnight into \nSaturday morning with more scattered to widespread showers and \nstorms spreading further north Saturday afternoon and evening, \nfavoring areas along and south of I-70. While the deep tropical\nmoisture has vacated the region, PWAT anomalies with this push \nof moisture are projected to be around 200 percent of normal, \nwhich while not as high as a few days ago, is still enough to \ncause issues and heavy rainfall. The main concern is that many \nareas are already saturated from previous rainfall and there is \nongoing flooding with many rivers and creeks running high. So \nany additional rainfall, especially where stronger convection \noccurs could result in more flooding and continuation of ongoing\nflooding. The rainfall amounts through Sunday are expected to \nrange from 0.5-1.0 inches along the highway 160 corridor with \nslightly higher amounts in the mountains and lower amounts \noutside of southwest Colorado. While we could see gusty winds \nand hail with a few storms, the main concern is heavy rainfall.\nHi res CAMs appear to show convection winding down Saturday \nnight with some isolated showers lingering. Showers become more \nisolated to scattered on Sunday as the low pressure lifts \noverhead, but these showers are expected to be lighter than \nprevious few days. Conditions begin to dry out Monday as this \nlow lifts northeast over the Plains.\n\nDRYING OUT NEXT WEEK:\n\nAs we move towards next week, models are also in fairly good \nagreement that dry zonal flow will begin to propagate through the\nregion by Monday. Because of this, we will be seeing cooler \nthan normal temperatures, with the potential for overnight lows \nto drop below freezing for a couple hours at a time. Meanwhile, \na low pressure system looks to enter the Pacific Northwest with \na few shortwaves moving across the Northern Rockies through the \nweek potentially bringing light showers to the north but \noverall, conditions appear to dry out until late next week when \nshowers potentially return as a shortwave trough moves through \nthe Desert SW and 4 Corners region pulling in some Pacific \nmoisture. Keep in mind, this is still relatively far out, so \nconfidence remains low on this matter as model solutions are \nvaried.",
